**Pattern 1: Static, immutable assets (versioned URLs)**

```java
@GetMapping("/assets/main.{hash}.js")
public ResponseEntity<Resource> js(@PathVariable String hash) {
    Resource r = assetService.load("main." + hash + ".js");
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(365, TimeUnit.DAYS).immutable().cachePublic())
        .contentType(MediaType.parseMediaType("application/javascript"))
        .body(r);
}
```

Asset URL includes content hash; if content changes, URL changes. Cache for 1 year; `immutable` tells browsers not to revalidate even on refresh.

**Pattern 2: API responses with ETag**

```java
@GetMapping("/products/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Product> getProduct(
        @PathVariable Long id,
        WebRequest request) {

    Product p = productService.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    String etag = "\"" + p.getVersion() + "-" + p.getUpdatedAt().toEpochMilli() + "\"";

    if (request.checkNotModified(etag)) {
        return null;  // 304 auto-returned by Spring
    }

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES).cachePublic())
        .eTag(etag)
        .body(p);
}
```

Spring's `WebRequest.checkNotModified(etag)` compares with `If-None-Match` header; returns 304 if match and returns null means Spring will send 304.

**Pattern 3: Conditional with Last-Modified**

```java
@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<Order> getOrder(
        @PathVariable String id,
        WebRequest request) {

    Order o = orderService.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    long lastModified = o.getUpdatedAt().toEpochMilli();

    if (request.checkNotModified(lastModified)) {
        return null;
    }

    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
            .staleWhileRevalidate(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
            .cachePrivate())
        .lastModified(lastModified)
        .body(o);
}
```

`cachePrivate()` because order is per-user; only browser cache, never CDN.

**Pattern 4: Auto-ETag for all responses**

```java
@Bean
public FilterRegistrationBean<ShallowEtagHeaderFilter> shallowEtagHeaderFilter() {
    FilterRegistrationBean<ShallowEtagHeaderFilter> filter = new FilterRegistrationBean<>();
    filter.setFilter(new ShallowEtagHeaderFilter());
    filter.addUrlPatterns("/api/*");
    return filter;
}
```

Spring hashes response body, sets ETag, handles conditional requests. Simple but has cost: must compute response before computing hash; saves bandwidth but not CPU. Good for static-ish API data; not great for expensive endpoints.

**Pattern 5: Stale-while-revalidate for low-volume dynamic data**

```java
ResponseEntity.ok()
    .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
        .staleWhileRevalidate(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
        .staleIfError(Duration.ofMinutes(30))
        .cachePublic())
    .body(data);
```

Cache serves for 30s fresh; during next 5 min, serves stale immediately while revalidating in background; if origin 5xx, serves stale for 30 min. Huge p99 wins.

**Pattern 6: Compression**

```yaml
server:
  compression:
    enabled: true
    mime-types: application/json,text/plain,text/html,text/css,text/javascript
    min-response-size: 1024
```

Spring Boot + Tomcat: gzip by default. For Brotli (better compression ratio), use a reverse proxy (nginx, Cloudflare) in front.

**Pattern 7: Cache-key awareness (Vary header)**

```java
ResponseEntity.ok()
    .header("Vary", "Accept, Accept-Language, Accept-Encoding")
    .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(10, TimeUnit.MINUTES).cachePublic())
    .body(data);
```

`Vary: Accept` means 'this response depends on Accept header'; CDN caches separately by header value. Without Vary, CDN may serve wrong representation to wrong client.

**Pattern 8: Per-user private data**

```java
// Never publicly cache; prevent CDN from storing user-specific
ResponseEntity.ok()
    .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS).cachePrivate())
    .header("Vary", "Authorization")
    .body(userData);
```

`private` + `Vary: Authorization` = browser caches per user; CDN doesn't.

// Compression at the Tomcat connector level is all-or-nothing by MIME type.
// For per-endpoint control (e.g. disable for a streaming endpoint), use a filter.

@Component
public class NoCompressionForStreamingFilter implements Filter {
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        var httpReq = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        if (httpReq.getRequestURI().startsWith("/v1/stream/")) {
            // Strip Accept-Encoding so Tomcat won't try to gzip SSE/NDJSON chunks
            chain.doFilter(new AcceptEncodingStripper(httpReq), res);
        } else {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        }
    }
}

// For Brotli (Spring Boot doesn't include it by default), add Jetty's Brotli module
// or a servlet filter based on brotli4j:
// implementation 'com.aayushatharva.brotli4j:brotli4j:1.16.0'
// implementation 'com.aayushatharva.brotli4j:native-linux-x86_64:1.16.0'

@Bean
FilterRegistrationBean<BrotliServletFilter> brotliFilter() {
    var reg = new FilterRegistrationBean<>(new BrotliServletFilter());
    reg.addUrlPatterns("/api/*");
    reg.setOrder(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 50);
    return reg;
}
